[{"id":"VRCE-1-61-1","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":1,"english":"O foremost of men, taking Sunaḥsepa, that illustrious king at noon rested in Puşkara, O descendant of Raghu.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.836,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061001","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6ee461b678c6a07ab7f45ecd9045b7b5805a64417d9596d310ae41fee28ffd52","compact":"e15cfc410e9928b14985cc675110d1bb3bc21f49d283bb25e869a16016ac8d96","nasal":"0ae36c8285c5dfed1da4976638288c8bac5a2130de51177abad3eca0538f7a38"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-2","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":2,"english":"And having arrived at the excellent Puskara, as the king was resting, the famous Sunaḥsepa with an aggrieved heart saw his maternal uncle Viśvāmitra in company with some saints engaged in asceticism. Thereupon, with a woebegone countenance, and sore afflicted with fatigue and thirst, he, O Rāma, flung himself into (Viśvāmitra's) lap, and said.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.71,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061002","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"379a5b7c5b22ae3adf003b5dfe0d6c70da9fbc1037543374680577b3812260c6","compact":"e51f737935149926a103a8a53540b36d9f1ad79697c0e1393c47ce9eaad52553","nasal":"e51f737935149926a103a8a53540b36d9f1ad79697c0e1393c47ce9eaad52553"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-3","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":3,"english":"And having arrived at the excellent Puskara, as the king was resting, the famous Sunaḥsepa with an aggrieved heart saw his maternal uncle Viśvāmitra in company with some saints engaged in asceticism. Thereupon, with a woebegone countenance, and sore afflicted with fatigue and thirst, he, O Rāma, flung himself into (Viśvāmitra's) lap, and said. I have neither father, nor mother, nor relatives, nor friends anywhere. It therefore behoves you, O gentle one, to save me in the interests of virtue, O foremost of ascetics.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.444,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-2","DUTT-1-62-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061003","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b7d9f11821016a4e5e2ea274dad1ef3247c2d6b991b94ecf449b0ae98061e87a","compact":"a16de0afbaebbbc3cbf01cba2bd1aac546921a51f9e745184231f55706436ba5","nasal":"dbc177cd304b94501c4f2c276a4c924fa9ae4d84d03977ae82c3ad2220365030"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-4","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":4,"english":"I have neither father, nor mother, nor relatives, nor friends anywhere. It therefore behoves you, O gentle one, to save me in the interests of virtue, O foremost of ascetics.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.952,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061004","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6e7d615598fbebc9d66c73fc16d7f3f28dfeb883e33749cd307adc9861b80386","compact":"1a3fa6210281999a99226b86512b4113296a0c2125fbdd2db86f6fdc78911476","nasal":"8fac869e0c67072ff680b0ec32a69e25609c89d6199aae031314d242e03ddc8d"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-5","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":5,"english":"And, O best of men, you are the protector of all, and their refuge. May the king have his desire and may I at the same time, attaining long life, and undeteriorating, gain heaven, having performed meritorious austerities!","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.786,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061005","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7ebe455839cde422a6d22dc2b6af7f68f3159aff36298dfddaf36bff8ee189b6","compact":"a8b81cd5985ae8ab220cebb9f8ebbd481c686d91fd90ea940b8f9d668698a9ca","nasal":"a8b81cd5985ae8ab220cebb9f8ebbd481c686d91fd90ea940b8f9d668698a9ca"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-6","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":6,"english":"And, O best of men, you are the protector of all, and their refuge. May the king have his desire and may I at the same time, attaining long life, and undeteriorating, gain heaven, having performed meritorious austerities! Do you with a delighted heart become a lord to me that am without one. And, O righteous one, even as a father rescue a son, do you deliver me from this peril.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.556,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-4","DUTT-1-62-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061006","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9b1aa7000d302b680a351e381fd8e2a765c35a9c0f8a38817318b13780d6b42d","compact":"69acab0efb9f5fd2229dec69332abf607b4db8856741235272d5fee1162e35ef","nasal":"69acab0efb9f5fd2229dec69332abf607b4db8856741235272d5fee1162e35ef"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-7","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":7,"english":"Hearing his words, Viśvāmitra of mighty austerities, pacifying him by every means, spoke to this sons, saying,","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061007","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"cff9360528448835e6a0f0863f0b8ad07fd09b4f9992f92e2c1bba447aa4297c","compact":"33378c2d7e1155770d705a8e73febf42ad9f0451e6afcc32efb14b0151a35da8","nasal":"61b4f38c3e3f2d29ee24fbef2612bfe99f887f83a43d36de1b86c4ecc62bd3d4"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-8","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":8,"english":"That in view of which fathers beget wellwishing sons, the compassing of other's welfare is at hand.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061008","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f55f5d5114c1e037d9eaf94cfa8617483f0a002c702cedfa9fa781d1d4a049aa","compact":"cbe7fc6502eee0eb3f82b7830384ffe37d40a726a2c2f8e81792bed17dff9a39","nasal":"6a66571dd8fde1451c0799fb37e11a2dd03216d784703d9e19304260d787376a"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-9","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":9,"english":"This youthful son. of the ascetic crave my protection. O you sons, do accomplish my desire by saving his life.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061009","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"4f6acbb1d3aca195412187a05c17b8d9b95aad571024667b008b240436b9251e","compact":"5ab8433a06e0f83cdee4d2fee71b7293a5012637b1838024b367796c5fb003fe","nasal":"5ab8433a06e0f83cdee4d2fee71b7293a5012637b1838024b367796c5fb003fe"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-10","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":10,"english":"You are all of virtuous deeds, you are all engaged in the observance of righteousness, do you confer satisfaction upon Agni by one of you becoming the (sacrificial) beast of the lord of men.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061010","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"eb910afde8da47af1c9434b532ca8cca45b42379ce0ce6a79c3828abf35463a5","compact":"c1fc7fcc8c04d40ffdfe98820581778bba31b9f8e41e669e939ae2e77c0f2b97","nasal":"097e56c52367ca9e832894c1d5f4fad3d5958eae1395bd7644821226c301cf8f"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-11","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":11,"english":"Thus Sunaḥsepa will obtain protection, the sacrifice will be freed from hindrance the deities will be propitiated, and finally my word will be made good.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061011","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d768e3b0aad6f00381648c921b3248e0612ce1fab944d550369edd58f9e44f45","compact":"e880f0fefaef864e00a65072921bf7e086c4e76409d24c502d006acfda635d42","nasal":"e880f0fefaef864e00a65072921bf7e086c4e76409d24c502d006acfda635d42"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-12","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":12,"english":"Hearing the ascetic's words, his sons, Madhucchanda and others, O foremost of me, haughtily and tauntingly answered.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.714,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061012","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"92a9c1fb532f3fc26b8d573b9656d6e85b5b3e2833687bb6a60137cca59e210e","compact":"12a4da4c75f830f2213abc8f2e81724cbfa18ec82a160179406b8d7e890a4993","nasal":"0572739b77fc148001492c06f8427838237229c3ac2a63265636110da07454b5"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-61-13","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":61,"verse":13,"english":"O lord, how, neglecting your own sons, you desire to deliver that of another? This we consider as heinous, even like to eating one's own flesh.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-62-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1061013","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b745f7662f4dd30ffa39609503393a280e9cc29f92b9f91bf5d2e8b1ae5f1988","compact":"ec6d2e31ca5d8216fe9bb61dbbfcf1aa759e93396584ea0d3470b308